<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muscogee</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Creek people, Muscogee Creek, Creek, Creek Confederacy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Muscogee, or Creek, are a Native American people of the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, whose towns in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> formed a powerful confederacy in the 18th century. They traded and fought with English, Spanish and French colonists. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/creek-war">Creek War</a> of 1813–1814 they lost much land, and most were removed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a> in the 1830s, where the Muscogee Nation is based.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muscogee.jpg" alt="Muscogee" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hodge, Frederick Webb (editor). Handbook of American Indians North of Mexico in Two Parts: Part I. Smithsonian Instit... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Creek_man_p_363_from_Handbook_of_American_Indians_north_of_Mexico_by_Hodge.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
